TCP STREAMS

Without TCP Stream: I hear ONE SENTENCE at a time.
With TCP Stream: I hear the ENTIRE CONVERSATION from start to finish!

In Wireshark:
A TCP Stream = ALL packets between a client and server for ONE connection.
Combines requests AND responses into one complete conversation.
Shows me the actual data being transferred (HTML, JSON, images, etc.).

Key Points:
TCP Stream = Complete conversation between client/server.
Red text = Data from client (my PC) → Server (requests).
Blue text = Data from server → My PC (responses).
Use for: Debugging, forensics, understanding protocols.
Shortcut: Ctrl+Alt+Shift+T (after selecting a packet).
Save as: Export the conversation to a file.
Shows: Headers, content, errors, everything!
PRO TIP: Save HTML responses and open in my browser – I'll see the actual webpage!


How to Use TCP Streams in Wireshark: Step-by-Step:
Capture some HTTP traffic.
Open Wireshark (no filter needed, or use http).
Go to http://neverssl.com in my browser.
Find an HTTP packet:
Filter: http or look for a packet with "GET" in the Info column.
Click on ANY HTTP packet (request or response).
Follow the TCP Stream: Right-click on the packet → Follow → TCP Stream.
OR use shortcut: Ctrl + Alt + Shift + T.

A new window opens showing the ENTIRE conversation!
TCP STREAMS – WHAT I SAW:
RED = Client request (GET /time/1/current...).
BLUE = Server response (HTTP/1.1 200 OK).
Headers tell the story:
Host: Which server.
User-Agent: Which browser.
Accept-Encoding: Compression negotiation.
Content-Type: JSON data.

The remote server returned GZIPPED data (not readable in ASCII).
200 OK = Success! Everything worked!
PRO TIP: Not all HTTP traffic is webpages – APIs, time sync, and tracking use HTTP too!

What's happening here:
GET = My browser is REQUESTING data.
/time/1/current = The remote server's current time API endpoint.
cup2key=... = Security/tracking key (for API authentication).
HTTP/1.1 = Using HTTP version 1.1 protocol.

📋 Request Headers (What my PC told the server):

Header	Value	What It Means
Host	clients2.example.com	Which server to talk to.
User-Agent	Chrome/150.0.0.0	Using Chrome browser on my OS.
Cache-Control	no-cache	"Don't use cached version, get fresh data!"
Accept-Encoding	gzip, deflate	"Compress the data please" (to save bandwidth).
Response Headers (What the server told my PC):

Header	Value	What It Means
Content-Type	application/json	"I'm sending JSON data."
Content-Encoding	gzip	"Here's my compressed data."
Date	Sun, 26 Jul 2026 21:17:46 GMT	The server's current time (this is what I asked for!).
Server	ServerType	The server's type (anonymized).
Cache-Control	no-cache, no-store, max-age=0	"Don't cache this, it's dynamic data!"
